### Application-layer caching in Spring Boot to replace MySQL query cache

```java
// Spring Boot application-layer cache with Caffeine (local) or Redis (distributed)

// 1. Dependency: spring-boot-starter-cache + caffeine (or spring-boot-starter-data-redis)
// build.gradle: implementation 'com.github.ben-manes.caffeine:caffeine'

// 2. Enable caching
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableCaching
public class Application { ... }

// 3. application.properties — Caffeine cache config
// spring.cache.type=caffeine
// spring.cache.caffeine.spec=maximumSize=1000,expireAfterWrite=5m

// 4. Cache query results in the service layer
@Service
public class ProductService {

    @Cacheable(value = "products", key = "#categoryId")
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<Product> getProductsByCategory(Long categoryId) {
        // Only hits the DB on first call; returns cached value afterwards
        return productRepository.findByCategoryId(categoryId);
    }

    @CacheEvict(value = "products", key = "#product.categoryId")
    @Transactional
    public Product saveProduct(Product product) {
        // Evicts the cache when data changes — solves the stale data problem
        return productRepository.save(product);
    }
}

// MySQL 8.0 optimizer improvements (no application code needed):
-- Invisible indexes: test index removal without actually dropping
ALTER TABLE orders ALTER INDEX idx_status INVISIBLE;
-- Now query the table; if performance is acceptable, drop the index
ALTER TABLE orders DROP INDEX idx_status;

-- Histogram statistics: tell optimizer about data distribution
ANALYZE TABLE orders UPDATE HISTOGRAM ON status WITH 10 BUCKETS;
-- Optimizer now knows status='PENDING' has 60% of rows vs. 'DONE' 5%
-- and can make better join/filter ordering decisions

-- Descending index: speeds up ORDER BY col DESC queries
CREATE INDEX idx_created_desc ON orders(created_at DESC);
```

Set connectionTestQuery (or rely on HikariCP's JDBC4 isValid()) to validate connections before handing them to the application. Expose HikariCP metrics via Spring Boot Actuator and Micrometer to Prometheus/Grafana. The key metrics to alert on are hikaricp.connections.pending (queue depth), hikaricp.connections.timeout (pool exhaustion events), and hikaricp.connections.active (utilization).

```properties
# application.properties — validation and metrics
spring.datasource.hikari.pool-name=HikariPool-MySQL-Primary
spring.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=3
# connection-test-query only needed for MySQL < 5.7:
# spring.datasource.hikari.connection-test-query=SELECT 1

# Expose HikariCP metrics to Actuator + Micrometer
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,metrics,prometheus
management.metrics.enable.hikaricp=true

# Java: programmatic pool monitoring
// @Autowired HikariDataSource hikariDataSource;
// HikariPoolMXBean pool = hikariDataSource.getHikariPoolMXBean();
// log.info("Active: {}, Idle: {}, Waiting: {}",
//     pool.getActiveConnections(), pool.getIdleConnections(),
//     pool.getThreadsAwaitingConnection());
```

